september is national suicide prevention  awareness month devoted to raise  awareness about suicide prevention  reduce the stigma around the topic and  encourage those in need to seek  resources and now more than ever that  includes our first  responders these agencies just like our  homes that of your public that's  watching right now they've sustained  losses from covid their families have  been affected by covid the pandemic has  changed their lives just as much as it's  changed ours all the while still having  to respond to these natural disasters  like ida  um  you know  their own day-to-day pressure of living  and the crisis of 9-1-1 calls one after  the other after the other which is the  nature of this type of work brandi lee  baker is the president and co-founder of  the code 9 project a peer support  hotline comprised of active and former  military law enforcement and first  responders dedicated to helping bring  suicide numbers down by providing  support and resources  she says the coronavirus pandemic is  putting first responders and health care  workers at a high risk for mental health  problems as they're at the front lines  providing care to others while  struggling with their own ptsd and  depression her team was recently  deployed to a department in louisiana  that experienced a department suicide  and reached out for help brandi lee  wants every agency nationwide to know  they can call her non-profit  organization day or night for help and  can find out more at thecode9project.org
 

